From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nomorecoolnicknames <nomorecoolnicknames@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 18:18:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] libc: treat a null printf format as empty

Legacy MediaTek vendor code calls snprintf(dst, n, nullptr, arg) as an
idiom (libmal_simmngr.so does it at ten call sites). On Nougat the format
went through mbrtowc, which accepts null, and produced an empty string;
this implementation dereferences it and kills the process (mtkmal on the
Meizu MX6). A null format is undefined behaviour, so treating it as empty
only weakens what faults.

Change-Id: Ia6992a825b9ea479b9a2cd494cd2267467a80535

diff --git a/libc/stdio/vfprintf.cpp b/libc/stdio/vfprintf.cpp
index d99d09c..9ebce99 100644
--- a/libc/stdio/vfprintf.cpp
+++ b/libc/stdio/vfprintf.cpp
@@ -44,6 +44,19 @@
 
 int FUNCTION_NAME(FILE* fp, const CHAR_TYPE* fmt0, va_list ap) {
   int caller_errno = errno;
+  // A null format is undefined behaviour, and legacy vendor code relies on what
+  // the old implementation happened to do with it. MediaTek's Nougat blobs call
+  // snprintf(dst, n, nullptr, arg) as a routine idiom -- libmal_simmngr.so alone
+  // at ten call sites -- and on Nougat that was harmless: the scanner ran the
+  // format through mbrtowc, which accepts a null pointer, and left just a
+  // terminator. This implementation scans the format directly, so the same call
+  // dereferences null and kills the process (mtkmal while enabling the SIM/IMS
+  // side, Meizu MX6). Treat null as an empty format: it restores the behaviour
+  // those binaries were built against and only weakens what this faults on.
+  // An array of the right character type, not a cast literal: this file is
+  // compiled for both char and wchar_t.
+  static const CHAR_TYPE kEmptyFormat[1] = { 0 };
+  if (fmt0 == nullptr) fmt0 = kEmptyFormat;
   int n, n2;
   CHAR_TYPE* cp;            /* handy char pointer (short term usage) */
   CHAR_TYPE sign;           /* sign prefix (' ', '+', '-', or \0) */
